  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA warm frontal boundary will push toward the region this\nafternoon and evening. This will result in increasing clouds, \nand eventually rain showers moving into the area. The majority \nof the area will likely be rain-free during the daytime on \nSaturday, but a few showers may sneak in west of I-81 during the\nafternoon.\n\nThe most concentrated period of rain showers will be Saturday \nnight/early Sunday morning. PWATs will be around 1.7 inches and \ncoupled with a decent LLJ, locally heavy rainfall will be \npossible. The greatest threat for this will be across Northeast \nPA-Catskills. While widespread flash flooding is not \nanticipated, cannot rule out some localized flooding issues late\nSaturday night.\n\nThere will likely be a bit of a lull in the rainfall Sunday\nmorning, before a cold front will bring another round of\nscattered showers and thunderstorms in the afternoon. Hi-res\nguidance is now showing a fair amount of destabilzation with \n1,000-1,500 J/KG of surface CAPE and close to 1,000 J/KG of \nDCAPE. However, poor lapse rates look to keep the threat for \nsevere thunderstorms more isolated with gusty winds as the main \nconcern. \n \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nFollowing the passage of the cold front, cooler air will move\ninto the region, which will bring highs in the 60s to lower 70s\nboth Monday and Tuesday. Would not be surprised if Monday ends \nup cloudier than forecasted given northwesterly flow. High \npressure though will keep conditions dry during this period. \nAnother system may bring some scattered showers and potentially \nsome thunderstorms by the middle of next week, along with warmer\ntemperatures.",
